# Update pushes include a release

- A request to push a Shrapwill update includes publishing its matching GitHub
  release unless the user explicitly requests a source-only push. A code push
  alone does not complete the task.
- Check the latest published version and choose a new stable version. Never
  overwrite an existing published version's binaries to deliver new changes.
- Run `npm run release:version -- v<version>` from `Source` to update all five
  version files together. Include those changes in the pushed commit and tag.
- Build and validate the versioned installer and signed update packages. Keep
  the user's running editors and original media intact.
- Publish the installer, portable ZIP, portable update package, and signed
  `shrapwill-update.json` to the matching GitHub release. Upload the manifest
  last; prefer a draft until every artifact is ready.
- Verify the public release, tag/commit, asset sizes, and signed latest-update
  feed before reporting success. Distinguish a source push from a published
  release if access or validation blocks completion.
- Never commit or publish private signing keys, credentials, or local QA data.
  Use the normal permission-reviewed access route when restricted Git/network
  commands fail; do not assume other sessions have the same permissions.
- Follow `CONTRIBUTING.md` and `docs/updates.md` for the release procedure.
